00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.510
When I tend to
think of Cloud SQL,

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:54.660
I tend to think of the
technology stack that

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:56.320
goes into running a database.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.560
Many of you raised
your hands when I asked

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.040
are you managing MySQL today.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:02.498
So you know the stack or at
least some portion of the stack

00:06:02.498 --> 00:06:03.210
pretty well.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:06.600
The leftmost column in the
stack is the hardware column.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:10.950
This is racking and stacking
servers, thinking about power

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.570
and cooling for the
servers and data center.

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:15.540
As you move to the
left, the next stack

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.210
is the operating system itself,
keeping that operating system

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.052
up to date with patches.

00:06:20.052 --> 00:06:22.260
Move further right even than
that, and now we finally

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:23.310
get to the database.

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:25.350
Beyond installing
the database itself,

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:28.140
we've got to patch the
database, make sure it's secure,

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.290
and do things like taking
regular backups for data

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:32.560
protection.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:36.960
Finally rightmost on the diagram
is where things get interesting

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.900
and we get into some
advanced functionality, using

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:42.540
replication, for example,
for high availability,

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.000
or read scale-out, and
thinking about things

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.330
like, how do we determine if a
primary instance is unhealthy.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:49.710
What should we do
about failover?

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.860
Should it be a manual
task or an automatic task?

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:56.190
All of this then gets
wrapped into monitoring,

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:57.840
where we want to
make sure we know

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:00.210
what's going on with our stack.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:04.170
What Cloud SQL aims to do is
take this set of technology,

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:06.330
the technology required
to run a database,

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:08.710
and turn it into
a single API call

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:11.550
so that as a DevOps
engineer, a developer, a DBA,

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:13.950
with a single API
call, you can bring up

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:17.400
a very simple development
database or a much more

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.880
complex production database
with high availability,

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.030
automatic failover,
read scale-out.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:26.640
What is not shown
in this diagram

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.010
is what backs the
Cloud SQL service.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:32.010
And Jason from RealMassive,
another Cloud SQL user,

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:33.130
points this out.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:37.320
Cloud SQL is backed
by a 24 by 7 SRE team.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:39.270
These are the folks who
write the automation

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:42.930
and the monitoring to keep
Cloud SQL's fleet of databases

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:43.634
healthy.

00:07:43.634 --> 00:07:46.050
And when needed, they're the
folks who get on the keyboard

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:48.552
and bring an unhealthy
database back to life.

00:07:48.552 --> 00:07:50.010
Jason's pointing
out here that this

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:53.010
is one less thing for his team
to worry about so that they can

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:55.170
focus their attention
