00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.610
With that overview
of Cloud SQL in mind,

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.660
we'll get into
migration options.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:04.800
If you are running a
MySQL today and you

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.560
wish to migrate that
server, that MySQL database,

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:10.950
to Cloud SQL, you have
essentially a simple option

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:12.310
and a more advanced option.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:15.780
What I'll start by saying
is whenever possible,

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:17.880
take the simple approach.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:21.900
It works, and it's
straightforward operationally

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:22.890
to implement.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:24.810
The simple approach is this.

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:28.050
I have my running MySQL
instance, my server

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:30.450
that I'm running today
and managing myself.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:32.730
I set it to read only mode.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:36.440
My application now can no
longer write to the database.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:40.440
The database data itself
is frozen in time.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.659
I ask the database
to export that data,

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:45.970
or dump in MySQL terminology,
so I get all the data set,

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:47.490
which is currently frozen.

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:49.620
And when I have that, I
take it, and I upload it

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.480
to Google Cloud
Storage, for example,

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:53.340
as a way to get it
into Google Cloud.

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:56.460
Then I ask Cloud SQL,
import this data set.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:59.600
When Cloud SQL is done,
I repoint my application

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.400
at Cloud SQL and begin
accepting writes.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:03.810
It's a very simple approach.

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:07.710
There is one obvious consequence
of this approach, right?

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:09.120
Which is?

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:10.800
AUDIENCE: Downtime.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:14.070
BRETT HESTERBERG: Downtime,
and potentially a lots of it.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:17.340
There are ways to
reduce downtime

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:19.800
and where, if you're thinking
about a simple approach,

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:21.570
an application
that is reasonably

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:24.600
tolerant of downtime, you can
minimize the amount of time

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.300
you're down by really
focusing on how

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.160
to optimize the import time.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:32.260
Importing data into a database
can be very time consuming.

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:37.540
So this is showing three general
themes to optimize and import.

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:39.840
Let's start first with
optimizing your schema

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:40.800
for import.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:43.890
Here, it's all
about handling keys.

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:48.210
As a starting point, import your
data without secondary keys.

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:51.630
You can recreate indexes
after you've imported.

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:55.530
Second item here is import
your data in primary key order.

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:57.860
This has real consequences
when writing data

00:09:57.860 --> 00:10:00.160
or importing into a database.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:04.050
The second theme you see here
is optimize your Cloud SQL

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:06.170
instance for import.

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:09.510
Cloud SQL can be
scaled very small.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:10.950
It can also be
scaled very large.

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:12.480
And what I'm
recommending here is,

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.020
think about allocating
CPUs, and RAM,

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:18.450
and storage performance
to Cloud SQL

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:21.570
so that it has the
infrastructure, the resources,

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:24.300
to run fast during an import.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:28.020
Also note that while I think
scaling CPUs and RAM is very

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.780
straightforward-- more CPUs,
more RAM generally equals more

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:31.980
performance--

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:34.927
in Google Cloud, we
tie storage performance

00:10:34.927 --> 00:10:35.760
to storage capacity.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.967
So if you want more
IOPS, more capacity

00:10:38.967 --> 00:10:40.050
is how you get those IOPS.

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:43.050
If you know you have a
200-gigabyte database,

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:46.380
allocate 200 gigs or more
of storage to that instance

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:48.720
so that you immediately
get the IOPS.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.090
I say that because Cloud
SQL has an option that

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:55.740
allows you to tell Cloud SQL,
grow the disk on my behalf.

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:59.280
And you can start an import
of a 200-gigabyte database

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:00.840
with a 10-gigabyte disk.

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:03.950
Cloud SQL will happily grow
the disk incrementally for you

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.220
as you do the import.

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:08.580
What you won't get is the
performance of the 200-gig disk

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:10.990
until you reach
that capacity level.

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:13.440
And then lastly in
this theme, turn off

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:16.560
an advanced feature like binary
logging for the import, which

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:18.450
has a right
consequence, has a right

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:20.730
overhead associated with it.

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:23.640
Last theme here is know how
to monitor the import process.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:28.380
Cloud SQL comes with a set
of basic performance metrics

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:30.580
that we show in
the Cloud Console.

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:31.920
Know how to look at those.

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:33.344
And watch out for reads.

00:11:33.344 --> 00:11:34.260
We're doing an import.

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:35.820
We should be just
mostly writing.

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:37.590
If we're doing a
lot of reads, that

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:39.160
indicates the
database is paging.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:40.674
We're exhausting our buffer.

00:11:40.674 --> 00:11:42.840
And we're making the database
do a lot of extra work

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:44.190
during import.

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:47.100
So these three themes,
these three ways

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:49.680
to optimize your
import, can reduce

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:51.450
the amount of
downtime associated

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:53.560
with our simple migration.

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:55.890
One note here is
that after import, we

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:57.210
can undo some of these things.

00:11:57.210 --> 00:12:00.240
So I asked you to scale your
instance large for the import.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:02.490
I'm not saying you have to
keep it there indefinitely.

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:04.140
That costs money every month.

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:06.600
Cloud SQL allows you to
very easily to remove

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:08.710
CPUs and remove RAM.

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:10.530
So you don't have to
keep a large instance

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:12.150
in the long term.

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:14.790
Scaling at large just for import
can be a very cost effective

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:16.680
way to gain performance.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:18.720
After import, you can
turn on binary logging.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:21.330
Binary logging Cloud SQL uses
for some advanced features

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:24.152
like high availability,
point-in-time recovery, things

00:12:24.152 --> 00:12:25.360
that we think you should use.

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:27.960
So go ahead and turn
that on after import.

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:31.690
And then, of course, add
your indexes back as needed.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:34.300
We talked about disabling some
of that before the import.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:36.790
Bring those back now that
you've got your data there.

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:40.260
OK, those are general import
performance optimization steps

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:42.270
as a way to limit the
downtime associated

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:44.640
with our simple migration.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:47.220
Now, many of you saw right
through the simple migration

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:50.400
and said, that's great for
some non-critical apps I have.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:52.170
What about my critical
apps that aren't

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:53.340
very tolerant of downtime?

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:54.370
What do I do?

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:57.005
And that's really the
focus of our talk today.

00:12:57.005 --> 00:12:58.380
We'll go through
it conceptually.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:12:59.755
And then we're
going to do it all

00:12:59.755 --> 00:13:01.170
together live with
an application

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:03.150
that Gabby has written.

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:05.220
This diagram shows
how we're going

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:08.530
to minimize downtime today
with our live app migration.

00:13:08.530 --> 00:13:11.470
You have an application pointing
at your existing MySQL server.

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:12.720
It's doing reads and writes.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:14.700
And it's a live application.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:16.979
We're going to tell that
existing MySQL server,

00:13:16.979 --> 00:13:19.020
do an export of your data,
do a dump of your data

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:20.635
as it exists right now.

00:13:20.635 --> 00:13:22.920
The database starts
doing that export.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:25.470
We don't stop the database
from accepting new writes.

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:26.640
New writes are coming in.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:28.740
Updates and inserts
are coming in.

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:32.020
We get the export out, which
is now a little bit stale.

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:34.500
We send it off to
Google Cloud Storage.

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:36.510
And we import it into Cloud SQL.

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:39.180
This process may take minutes
if you're really lucky,

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:41.490
hours, which is
common, maybe even

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:43.110
days if you have a lot of data.

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:45.720
So you now have a
Cloud SQL instance that

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:47.580
has a data set that's stale.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:49.710
It doesn't have any of
the updates, inserts

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:52.170
that have been going on while
we're doing this process.

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:54.510
The upside is, our app
been up the whole time.

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:57.420
Our users are still happy.

00:13:57.420 --> 00:14:00.990
What we do here to catch
up the Cloud SQL instance

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:03.270
is set up a replication
relationship

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:08.280
between your existing
MySQL Server and Cloud SQL.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:10.866
We've called this external
server replication,

00:14:10.866 --> 00:14:13.240
where your external server is
still the primary instance.

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:16.060
It's still accepting new
reads and new writes.

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:18.240
And that's the important
bit here is the new writes.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:21.330
Cloud SQL, once it
begins to replicate

00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:24.680
from your external server,
is starting to catch up now.

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:27.180
It says, OK, I'm
four hours behind.

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:28.660
I've got some work to do.

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:29.760
And it begins to catch up.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:32.610
All the while, your
external MySQL server

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:34.620
continues to serve traffic.

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:37.350
So we've had no
downtime thus far.

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:39.810
Once we know our
Cloud SQL replica is

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:42.540
in sync with our
external MySQL server--

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:44.400
the two have the same data set.

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:47.640
The last transaction I wrote
here to my external server

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:49.170
is now in Cloud SQL--

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:50.910
now we can make our failover.

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:52.500
Now we can make our move.

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:55.200
We set our external
server to read only.

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:57.390
We say freeze the
data set as it is.

00:14:57.390 --> 00:15:00.060
We make sure that our Cloud
SQL replica has that data.

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:02.970
And then we break the
replication connection.

00:15:02.970 --> 00:15:05.880
Now, we repoint our
application to Cloud SQL.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:09.200
And we tell Cloud SQL,
become the primary instance.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:10.480
Start accepting writes.

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:12.470
You are now the
primary instance.

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:15.190
Our application begins
happily writing again.

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:17.200
And we've migrated
into Cloud SQL,

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:19.660
or failed over, if you
will, to the cloud,

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:20.980
with minimal downtime.

00:15:20.980 --> 00:15:24.490
The key here is
that this process

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:27.760
minimizes downtime for a
database of essentially

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:28.720
any size.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:31.660
What you saw here is we were
keeping our external MySQL

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:34.030
server, our old
primary, up as long

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:37.210
as we needed to make sure we
could get the data to Cloud SQL

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:39.770
and that Cloud SQL could
catch up via replication.

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:42.220
This works on large data
sets as well as small data

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:45.080
sets to limit downtime.

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:46.690
So it's one thing
to talk about this.

00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:49.750
But let's start to
see this in action.

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:53.980
Gabby has an application
based on a LAMP stack, so

00:15:53.980 --> 00:15:57.700
a very common stack that
we see folks using this

00:15:57.700 --> 00:16:01.660
as a single VM that
includes the MySQL database,

00:16:01.660 --> 00:16:04.090
Apache web server, PHP.

00:16:04.090 --> 00:16:06.400
And we happen to be
running what is becoming

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:08.770
a very popular WordPress site.

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:11.306
So Gabby's WordPress
site started small.

00:16:11.306 --> 00:16:13.180
She's now gotten a lot
of additional traffic.

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:15.940
And you'll see why
when you see this site.

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:18.370
She has decided to scale.

00:16:18.370 --> 00:16:23.500
She needs to take MySQL and
that workload off of this server

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:24.790
and send it to Cloud SQL.

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:26.470
So we've got our
current state here.

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:28.762
We are on a single GCE VM.

00:16:28.762 --> 00:16:30.220
One thing to note
here is that this

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:31.964
could be physical hardware.

00:16:31.964 --> 00:16:33.880
This could be a server
somewhere in the world.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:36.940
This could be a VM
in some other cloud.

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:40.210
The point here is that
Gabby has full control

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:42.790
over the database in this VM.

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:45.460
She desires to move
to a world where

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:48.490
she's offloaded all the MySQL
load to Cloud SQL, where

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:51.580
her VM now is
simply a web server

00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:54.970
with a connection to Cloud
SQL for its database needs.

00:16:54.970 --> 00:16:57.910
And we're going to get into
exactly how to do this.

00:16:57.910 --> 00:17:03.220
In fact, we're gonna
do it live on this URL.

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:05.530
Sorry about that for
the errant click.

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:08.452
If you wish to follow along, if
you want to see for yourself,

00:17:08.452 --> 00:17:10.660
what does downtime look
like, what does this app look

00:17:10.660 --> 00:17:13.930
like as we're doing this,
fire up your mobile device,

00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:15.700
browse to this URL.

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:18.579
What you'll find is Gabby
has created a WordPress site

00:17:18.579 --> 00:17:24.069
that has the listings of every
Google Cloud Next 2018 session.

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:25.839
So obviously, it's a hot site.

00:17:25.839 --> 00:17:28.300
And it's now struggling
under its own load.

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:29.950
You can watch us do this live.

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:32.330
We'd love to get your
traffic as we go along.

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:34.990
So make sure you see when
the site is down, exactly

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:37.100
for how long it's down,
and when it comes back up.

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:37.600
Gabby?

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:45.660
GABRIELA FERRARA: Just a moment.

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:46.670
Thank you, Brett.

00:17:52.912 --> 00:17:53.870
BRETT HESTERBERG: Wait.

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:58.570
Let me give the URL
up on the screen here.

00:17:58.570 --> 00:17:59.080
Oops.

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:00.640
Can you switch it back
just for a moment.

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:01.000
GABRIELA FERRARA: OK.

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:01.630
AUDIENCE: We can see it.

00:18:01.630 --> 00:18:02.280
GABRIELA FERRARA: You can see?

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:03.310
OK.

00:18:03.310 --> 00:18:06.650
So this is a WordPress
that I imported the data

00:18:06.650 --> 00:18:11.150
from all of the Google
Cloud Next sessions.

00:18:11.150 --> 00:18:15.770
For now, you can just
browse through it.

00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:16.580
It's working.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:20.030
It's on a single VM
on Compute Engine.

00:18:20.030 --> 00:18:25.160
And today, we're going to
migrate that MySQL server

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:27.830
from the VM to the Cloud SQL.

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:31.190
For purposes of
clarity, I'm going

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:35.060
to be calling primary
the server on the VM,

00:18:35.060 --> 00:18:38.660
and replica the one that we're
going to be setting up now.

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:46.440
So Brett showed you the new
menu on the SQL dashboard

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:47.630
on the console.

00:18:47.630 --> 00:18:54.044
So once you access the SQL menu,
you can see Migrate Data here.

00:18:54.044 --> 00:18:55.460
And as we said,
you're going to be

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:57.860
using the UI to be doing this.

00:18:57.860 --> 00:19:02.700
But you can also use
the API or the CLI.

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:04.610
Once you start to
begin migration,

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:06.050
you need a couple of data.

00:19:06.050 --> 00:19:09.870
And you need to have prepared
your server for this.

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:11.480
One of the things
that we require

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:16.940
is for you to have binary
logging on on your primary,

00:19:16.940 --> 00:19:23.300
and also have GTID enabled so we
know which last transaction was

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:25.070
executed on the server.

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:27.920
It wants to generate
the down point for it.

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:29.270
So I'm going to create it here.

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:32.060
I'm going to zoom in.

00:19:32.060 --> 00:19:33.740
So name of the
data set, I'm going

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:42.360
to put next2018-migration-d.

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:44.960
So the IP address that
I have for that website

00:19:44.960 --> 00:19:47.930
is the same VM from that.

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:51.930
That address is this one.

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:55.200
Yes, I'm copying.

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:57.740
Well, you also need to have
a replication user set up

00:19:57.740 --> 00:19:59.990
on your primary server
to have the permissions

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:02.600
necessary to copy all the data.

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:04.580
In this case, I created
a replication user

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:05.795
called Replication.

00:20:05.795 --> 00:20:08.170
And I'm going to click here
so you don't see my password.

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:15.740
Once you set up that, you need
to make sure that the database

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:18.240
version that you're using--

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:23.760
we are supporting MySQL
5.6 and 5.7 for migration.

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:27.800
So in my case, it's
MySQL 5.7 on the VM,

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:30.440
so I'm going to just
keep it as it is.

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:34.820
And if you use certificates
for SSL and TLS,

00:20:34.820 --> 00:20:38.240
you can set up in here
and on your certificate.

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:40.330
In my case, I'm not using it.

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:43.060
When you click on
Next, it's going

00:20:43.060 --> 00:20:47.172
to ask you for the name of the
replica you'll be creating.

00:20:47.172 --> 00:20:49.130
I'm going to keep the
name they proposed to me.

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:52.500
It's the same name
as the primary that's

00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:55.130
with called SQL added to it.

00:20:55.130 --> 00:20:57.600
Another important
thing is if you

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:01.400
know where your server
is, it's ideal for you

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:04.340
to get a database
server that's closer

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:09.380
to it, the same region, or even
the same zone, if possible.

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:12.630
In my case, if I look on
the Compute Engine instance,

00:21:12.630 --> 00:21:16.370
I am on US Central,
and my zone is F.

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:19.130
So the latency
doesn't get too high,

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:22.410
I'm going to keep
it on the same zone.

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:27.200
As Brett mentioned too, you
can scale up for this import.

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:31.310
And I'm going to choose a 2
CPU with 7 and 1/2 gigabytes

00:21:31.310 --> 00:21:32.960
of RAM.

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:37.610
The ideal proportion is if
you have a 5 gigabyte set,

00:21:37.610 --> 00:21:44.330
get at least instance with
at least 10 gigabytes so you

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:47.000
can load all the
memory and don't

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:48.180
have any problems with that.

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:55.530
As I said before, you need to
have GTID and binary logging

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:56.930
enabled.

00:21:56.930 --> 00:22:01.070
You need to generate a file on
MySQL Dump using the MySQL Dump

00:22:01.070 --> 00:22:02.310
comment.

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:05.060
And once you've
generated that file,

00:22:05.060 --> 00:22:07.980
you're going upload it to Google
Cloud Storage on a bucket.

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:09.660
The bucket's
private so you don't

00:22:09.660 --> 00:22:11.910
need to worry about security.

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:13.770
And you just choose
which file it

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:15.330
is that's going to be imported.

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:18.480
As I said before, this does
have the information's GTID

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:22.030
and binary logging that's going
to be used for replication.

00:22:24.950 --> 00:22:27.970
If you're going to be
connecting to this database

00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:30.630
with [INAUDIBLE]
called SQL proxy, which

00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:34.010
[INAUDIBLE] binary
application to be

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:36.260
transparent for
connection, it makes

00:22:36.260 --> 00:22:39.950
your application think
that the server is local,

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:42.710
you can also whitelist
an IP address.

00:22:42.710 --> 00:22:45.080
In this case, I'm going to
whitelist the IP address off

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:46.970
the VM data we were using.

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:48.440
So you need to give a name.

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:50.270
In this case it's Application.

00:22:50.270 --> 00:22:53.070
And I'm going to use the same
IP address as the database

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:54.670
because they are
on the same server.

00:22:57.662 --> 00:22:59.120
BRETT HESTERBERG:
So one point here

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:01.795
is that Cloud SQL comes
with a built-in firewall.

00:23:01.795 --> 00:23:04.580
And what Gabby's doing right
now is telling Cloud SQL's

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:08.360
firewall, allow a connection
from this particular address,

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:09.890
which is her application server.

00:23:09.890 --> 00:23:12.470
If we don't do this, Cloud
SQL's going to block the network

00:23:12.470 --> 00:23:13.180
connection.

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:15.070
GABRIELA FERRARA: Yeah,
you could, in theory,

00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:17.190
whitelist all IP
addresses on the internet.

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:19.485
We don't recommend
you to do that.

00:23:19.485 --> 00:23:22.110
BRETT HESTERBERG: Yeah, that is
an official non-recommendation.

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:24.276
GABRIELA FERRARA: We don't
recommend you to do that.

00:23:24.276 --> 00:23:27.080
But for this example
here, we're just

00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:29.490
going to whitelist the IP
off of the application.

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:32.050
Once you hit Create,
what's going to happen

00:23:32.050 --> 00:23:34.070
is we're going to start
to provision an IP

00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:37.890
address for this instance
that's being created.

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:40.160
This IP address that's
going to be created here,

00:23:40.160 --> 00:23:41.910
it's not the IP address
we're going to use

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:43.760
to connect to the instance.

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:45.410
It's the IP address
we're going to use

00:23:45.410 --> 00:23:48.440
to whitelist on
your primary server

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:51.650
through your firewall to allow
the connection to the database.

00:23:51.650 --> 00:23:54.710
So you don't need to
put all your server

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:56.930
through the whole
internet to see.

00:23:56.930 --> 00:24:00.300
So once that's
created, provision,

