00:29:36.389 --> 00:29:38.055
BRETT HESTERBERG:
Yeah, so while Gabby's

00:29:38.055 --> 00:29:40.900
doing the promotion again, I
think a couple key steps here.

00:29:40.900 --> 00:29:46.410
One is we validated that data
on our former primary server,

00:29:46.410 --> 00:29:50.610
our LAMP stack, exactly matched
that on our Cloud SQL replica.

00:29:50.610 --> 00:29:52.470
We made sure that
the two were in sync.

00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:56.340
Gabby did this by showing a
number of posts with WordPress.

00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:58.290
There are a good
many ways to do this.

00:29:58.290 --> 00:30:00.150
If you have a very
active application,

00:30:00.150 --> 00:30:02.430
you'll need to set
your external MySQL

00:30:02.430 --> 00:30:05.800
server in some read-only
mode so that the writes stop.

00:30:05.800 --> 00:30:07.220
Then you can begin the cut over.

00:30:07.220 --> 00:30:10.980
Gabby used a sed command
to substitute essentially

00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:13.380
the IP address of the
Cloud SQL instance

00:30:13.380 --> 00:30:16.510
in her WordPress config
file rather than local host

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:19.590
so that the application now
begins to shift and point

00:30:19.590 --> 00:30:20.970
to Cloud SQL.

00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:22.650
And then the key
step here was, what

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:25.650
Cloud SQL calls a
promotion, which is you

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:27.690
as an administrator
telling Cloud SQL, I want

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:30.390
to take this read replica,
which is read-only,

00:30:30.390 --> 00:30:33.060
and promote it to become
a primary instance,

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:35.300
meaning accepting
reads and writes.

00:30:35.300 --> 00:30:38.020
What Cloud SQL knows from
that point forward is,

00:30:38.020 --> 00:30:41.380
this instance is no longer a
follower of something else.

00:30:41.380 --> 00:30:43.920
This will accept writes
and blaze its own trail,

00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:44.420
so to speak.

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:45.336
GABRIELA FERRARA: Yes.

00:30:45.336 --> 00:30:45.890
Correct.

00:30:45.890 --> 00:30:47.715
And right now, its promoting.

00:30:47.715 --> 00:30:49.950
[LAUGHS]

00:30:49.950 --> 00:30:53.070
And you can see the application
with downtime right now.

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:55.470
So if you try to access
the application right now,

00:30:55.470 --> 00:30:59.790
it's having problems
accessing because it's not

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:01.800
finding the connection
with the database.

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:02.940
BRETT HESTERBERG: And this
is the cut over point.

00:31:02.940 --> 00:31:04.815
And I think you're seeing
it in the audience.

00:31:04.815 --> 00:31:06.360
Right now, the
application is down.

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:11.640
So this is when you would
schedule on the order of one

00:31:11.640 --> 00:31:15.090
to three minutes of
downtime for the migration.

00:31:15.090 --> 00:31:18.330
Gabby had shown you that
for the bulk of the process,

00:31:18.330 --> 00:31:21.360
getting the data to Cloud SQL
and setting up replication,

00:31:21.360 --> 00:31:22.770
your app is live.

00:31:22.770 --> 00:31:25.050
You can do these things
during business hours.

00:31:25.050 --> 00:31:28.590
When you're ready to cut over,
meaning when your application

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:31.050
is most tolerant of
downtime, then we

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:33.352
want to wrap up
with these steps.

00:31:33.352 --> 00:31:34.268
GABRIELA FERRARA: Yes.

00:31:34.268 --> 00:31:36.692
And I'm just waiting.

00:31:36.692 --> 00:31:38.400
BRETT HESTERBERG: And
the key to any demo

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:39.680
is that it ends up working here.

00:31:39.680 --> 00:31:41.580
GABRIELA FERRARA: Yeah,
that's the problem with those.

00:31:41.580 --> 00:31:43.170
BRETT HESTERBERG: Generally,
a promotion in Cloud SQL

00:31:43.170 --> 00:31:44.839
takes about one to two minutes.

00:31:44.839 --> 00:31:47.130
As Gabby mentioned, we're
doing some configuration work

00:31:47.130 --> 00:31:50.700
in MySQL itself, which
takes a bit of time,

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:53.190
and then we're doing some
configuration work in Cloud SQL

00:31:53.190 --> 00:31:57.030
to essentially move this
replica to a full blown primary

00:31:57.030 --> 00:31:58.000
instance.

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:00.810
One thing to note here while
we're watching the promotion

00:32:00.810 --> 00:32:05.250
is that the external server
technology we've released

00:32:05.250 --> 00:32:08.850
allows for the reverse
replication to happen as well,

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:12.720
meaning we can, as a
Cloud SQL primary server,

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:15.360
replicate to your
external server as well.

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:18.300
So effectively, you're
able to fail into Cloud SQL

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:20.040
and fail back out.

00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:22.870
In case of a problem
with this migration path,

00:32:22.870 --> 00:32:26.100
for example, you may wish
to re-promote your existing

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:28.020
server back to primary.

00:32:28.020 --> 00:32:31.650
And this gives you,
essentially, an out when you're

00:32:31.650 --> 00:32:32.830
going through this process.

00:32:32.830 --> 00:32:34.650
We think it's
important to note this

00:32:34.650 --> 00:32:37.950
because we want to make sure
that migration to Cloud SQL

00:32:37.950 --> 00:32:41.130
is as easy in as it is out.

00:32:41.130 --> 00:32:43.690
Cloud SQL compatible and open.

00:32:43.690 --> 00:32:48.000
And we want to make sure that
you have as many on ramps

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:50.180
as you do off
ramps, so to speak.

00:32:50.180 --> 00:32:51.138
GABRIELA FERRARA: Yeah.

