00:36:03.630 --> 00:36:06.295
You can migrate off of Cloud
SQL in this way as well.

00:36:06.295 --> 00:36:08.520
A few next steps for you all.

00:36:08.520 --> 00:36:10.550
As I mentioned at
the beginning, use

00:36:10.550 --> 00:36:14.220
a simple approach for
migration whenever possible.

00:36:14.220 --> 00:36:16.440
That will be for
non-critical applications

00:36:16.440 --> 00:36:18.870
that are reasonably
tolerant of downtime.

00:36:18.870 --> 00:36:20.760
Think about reducing
that downtime

00:36:20.760 --> 00:36:23.010
by optimizing your import.

00:36:23.010 --> 00:36:24.450
Generally, optimizing
your import,

00:36:24.450 --> 00:36:27.510
whether you're using the
simple downtime migration

00:36:27.510 --> 00:36:30.300
scheme or the more
advanced low downtime

00:36:30.300 --> 00:36:32.520
migration that we
just demonstrated,

00:36:32.520 --> 00:36:36.780
optimizing that import will
be wise in either case.

00:36:36.780 --> 00:36:39.330
To get started with Cloud
SQL, if you're not already

00:36:39.330 --> 00:36:43.200
using Google Cloud, I recommend
starting with a free trial.

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:44.970
You get 300 free
trial dollars, which

00:36:44.970 --> 00:36:48.120
can be used on any product
in GCP, including Cloud SQL.

00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:51.260
It's a great way to start
to learn GCP and Cloud

00:36:51.260 --> 00:36:52.860
SQL as well.

00:36:52.860 --> 00:36:55.344
A couple presentation
pics for you.

00:36:55.344 --> 00:36:56.760
We are on Day 2
of the conference.

00:36:56.760 --> 00:36:59.650
We've got some, I think, great
sessions tomorrow as well.

00:36:59.650 --> 00:37:03.900
These are ones that caught my
eye, as well as Gabby's eye.

00:37:03.900 --> 00:37:06.720
We have one about Reinventing
Databases for Your Journey

00:37:06.720 --> 00:37:07.350
to the Cloud.

00:37:07.350 --> 00:37:09.660
I think those of you who
are interested in Spanner,

00:37:09.660 --> 00:37:12.510
and more generally, our
database portfolio at Google

00:37:12.510 --> 00:37:14.820
would enjoy this session.

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:18.170
For one about how to
think like an SRE,

00:37:18.170 --> 00:37:20.010
how to use Google
SRE for availability,

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:22.600
reliability, scalability
in hybrid environments,

00:37:22.600 --> 00:37:25.100
this one is really appropriate,
especially where many of you

00:37:25.100 --> 00:37:27.630
are contemplating
migrations to Google Cloud.

00:37:27.630 --> 00:37:29.024
Google helped invent SRE.

00:37:29.024 --> 00:37:30.690
And I think that
session will have a lot

00:37:30.690 --> 00:37:33.485
to share around how we think
about these things in Cloud.

00:37:33.485 --> 00:37:35.880
The last one on the
list, a tongue twister,

00:37:35.880 --> 00:37:38.040
Computing with
Power not Previously

00:37:38.040 --> 00:37:41.430
Possible with BigQuery
and Compute Engine--

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:43.860
this one really
describes what you

00:37:43.860 --> 00:37:47.490
can do at big scale in
Google Cloud, lots of CPUs,

00:37:47.490 --> 00:37:50.760
and with an application
like BigQuery.

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:53.220
With that, on behalf
of Gabby, I want

00:37:53.220 --> 00:37:55.800
to thank you all for spending
a chunk of your afternoon

00:37:55.800 --> 00:37:56.670
with us.

00:37:56.670 --> 00:37:58.290
And we'd love to
open up to questions.

00:37:58.290 --> 00:37:59.100
Thank you.

